{
  "dom_id": "step-q102710",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Consider the surface ${e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=e-3.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,1,-1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a-b}{c}?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=e-3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =(y{e}^{xy}-2x)i+(x{e}^{xy})j+(-4z)k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,-1) & =((1){e}^{(1)(1)}-2(1))\\text{ }i+(1){e}^{(1)(1)}\\text{ }j+(-4)(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(e-2)\\text{ }i+e\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨e-2,\\,e,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨e-2,\\,e,\\,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a-b}{c}=\\frac{(e-2)-e}{4}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
